<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head><%- include('../partials/head') %></head>
<body>
<div class="app">
<%- include('../partials/sidebar') %>
<%- include('../partials/topbar') %>

  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-body">
      <p class="hint mt-0">
        <strong>Every tracking element on one board</strong> — crawlability and indexation, Core Web Vitals,
        speed and TTFB, SSL and security headers, redirect chains, canonicals, structured data, JavaScript
        rendering, server-side crawl signals, mobile usability, titles and meta descriptions, heading structure,
        content quality and cannibalisation, internal linking, image optimisation, URL structure, and AI
        crawler access. Each check stores its value <em>and</em> its verdict, so the board answers "did something
        break" rather than only "what is it now".
      </p>
      <p class="hint" style="margin-bottom:0">
        A check that cannot measure returns <strong>unknown</strong>, never <strong>good</strong>. Collapsing
        those two is how a monitoring system reports green through an outage, so they are coloured differently
        and unknown metrics are excluded from the board score on both sides of the ratio.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <%- include('_partials/brandpicker') %>

  <% if (brand) { %>
  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-head"><h3>Run a sweep</h3></div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <form method="POST" action="/ai-seo/monitoring/run">
        <input type="hidden" name="brand_id" value="<%= brand.id %>">
        <div class="field-row">
          <label class="field">
            <span>Scope</span>
            <select name="scope">
              <option value="sample" selected>Sampled — a deliberate 12-URL cross-section</option>
              <option value="sitewide">Sitewide — every URL in the sitemap and every page with traffic</option>
            </select>
          </label>
          <label class="field">
            <span>Sample size for page-scoped checks</span>
            <select name="sample_size">
              <option value="6">6 — fast</option>
              <option value="12" selected>12 — default</option>
              <option value="20">20</option>
              <option value="30">30 — slow</option>
            </select>
          </label>
          <label class="field">
            <span>Sitewide cap</span>
            <select name="sitewide_cap">
              <option value="500">500 URLs</option>
              <option value="1500">1,500 URLs</option>
              <option value="3000" selected>3,000 URLs</option>
              <option value="10000">10,000 URLs — slow</option>
            </select>
          </label>
        </div>
        <p class="hint mt-0">
          <strong>A sample answers "is there a problem"; it cannot answer "which pages".</strong>
          Sampled scope runs every check against the same deliberate 12-URL cross-section — the homepage, the
          highest-impression pages, and one page per sitemap section — which is enough to detect a templating
          fault and cheap enough to run nightly.
          Sitewide scope additionally hands the FULL URL set to the checks that can absorb it: broken pages,
          canonical health and title/meta duplication. The rest keep the sample, because running PageSpeed
          against three thousand URLs would exhaust the daily quota on the first brand. Each check on the result
          page says which set it actually used, so a count is never read against the wrong denominator.
        </p>

        <p class="hint mt-0">
          <strong>How the sample is chosen, and why it matters.</strong> The homepage always, then the
          highest-impression pages from Search Console, then one page per distinct sitemap section. That last
          part is what makes it <em>representative</em> rather than merely important: a templating fault usually
          affects one section, and a sample drawn only from top pages would miss a broken section entirely
          because it has no traffic yet. The composition is recorded on every run, so "3 pages have no H1" is
          never mistaken for "3 pages on the whole site".
        </p>

        <div class="field">
          <label>Restrict to specific checks <span class="muted">(leave all unticked for a full sweep)</span></label>
          <% grouped.forEach(function (g) { %>
            <div style="margin-bottom:8px">
              <div class="nav-section" style="padding-left:0"><%= g.group %></div>
              <div class="checkbox-row" style="flex-wrap:wrap;gap:10px">
                <% g.items.forEach(function (c) {
                     const av = availability.find(function (a) { return a.key === c.key; }); %>
                  <label class="check" style="min-width:260px" title="<%= c.whatItTracks %>">
                    <input type="checkbox" name="checks" value="<%= c.key %>" <%= av && av.available ? '' : 'disabled' %>>
                    <%= c.element %>
                    <% if (av && !av.available) { %>
                      <span class="muted">— needs <%= av.missing.map(function (m) { return m.label; }).join(', ') %></span>
                    <% } %>
                  </label>
                <% }) %>
              </div>
            </div>
          <% }) %>
        </div>

        <div class="btn-row">
          <button type="submit" class="btn" <%= perms.canWrite ? '' : 'disabled' %>>Run sweep</button>
        </div>
        <p class="hint">
          Checks run one after another, not in parallel: they hit the same origin, the same PageSpeed quota and
          the same small memory allowance on this host, and fourteen at once is how a sweep gets rate-limited
          into reporting "unknown" for half the board. A full sweep at the default sample size takes a few minutes.
        </p>
      </form>
    </div>
  </div>

  <% if (board && board.latest && board.latest.result && board.latest.result.byGroup) { %>
  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-head">
      <h3>Latest sweep</h3>
      <span class="muted" style="font-size:12px"><%= fmtDateTime(board.latest.finished_at) %></span>
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <div class="grid cols-4">
        <% board.latest.result.byGroup.forEach(function (g) { %>
          <div class="setup-row">
            <span class="setup-mark">
              <% if (g.status === 'good') { %>&#10003;
              <% } else if (g.status === 'fail') { %>&#10007;
              <% } else if (g.status === 'warn') { %>!
              <% } else { %>?<% } %>
            </span>
            <div class="setup-body">
              <strong><%= g.group %></strong>
              <div class="muted" style="font-size:11px">
                <%= g.checks %> check<%= g.checks === 1 ? '' : 's' %><%= g.failing ? ' · ' + g.failing + ' failing' : '' %>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        <% }) %>
      </div>
      <div class="btn-row" style="margin-top:14px">
        <a class="btn secondary sm" href="/ai-seo/monitoring/<%= board.latest.id %>">Open the full board &rarr;</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <% } %>

  <% if (board && board.metrics && board.metrics.length) { %>
  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-head">
      <h3>Current metric values</h3>
      <span class="muted" style="font-size:12px">Newest capture per metric</span>
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <div class="table-wrap scroll-y" style="max-height:560px">
        <table class="data">
          <thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>URL</th><th>Value</th><th>Verdict</th><th>Captured</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
          <% board.metrics.forEach(function (m) { %>
            <tr>
              <td><code><%= m.metric_key %></code></td>
              <td class="truncate muted" style="max-width:220px"><%= m.url ? shortUrl(m.url, 44) : 'site-wide' %></td>
              <td><%= m.value == null ? '—' : m.value %></td>
              <td>
                <span class="badge <%= m.status === 'good' ? 'good' : (m.status === 'warn' ? 'medium' : (m.status === 'fail' ? 'critical' : 'neutral')) %>">
                  <%= m.status || 'unknown' %>
                </span>
              </td>
              <td class="muted"><%= fmtDateTime(m.captured_at) %></td>
              <td class="muted" style="font-size:11px"><%= m.detail || '—' %></td>
            </tr>
          <% }) %>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="hint" style="margin-bottom:0">
        Each row's history is available as JSON at
        <code>/ai-seo/monitoring/series/&lt;metric&gt;?brand=<%= brand.id %></code> — which is also what the
        alert engine reads when it decides a metric has regressed.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <% } %>
  <% } %>

  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-head"><h3>What each element tracks, and why</h3></div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <% grouped.forEach(function (g) { %>
        <h4><%= g.group %></h4>
        <div class="table-wrap"><table class="data">
          <thead><tr><th style="width:22%">Element</th><th style="width:30%">What it tracks</th><th style="width:34%">Why it matters</th><th>Scope</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
          <% g.items.forEach(function (c) { const av = availability.find(function (a) { return a.key === c.key; }); %>
            <tr>
              <td>
                <strong><%= c.element %></strong>
                <% if (av && !av.available) { %>
                  <div><span class="badge neutral">needs <%= av.missing.map(function (m) { return m.label; }).join(', ') %></span></div>
                <% } %>
              </td>
              <td class="muted"><%= c.whatItTracks %></td>
              <td class="muted"><%= c.whyItMatters %></td>
              <td><span class="badge neutral"><%= c.scope === 'page' ? 'sampled pages' : 'site-wide' %></span></td>
            </tr>
          <% }) %>
          </tbody>
        </table></div>
      <% }) %>
    </div>
  </div>

  <%- include('_partials/runs') %>

<%- include('../partials/sidebar-end') %>
<%- include('../partials/foot') %>
